{"ratings":[{"id":"F1","consequence":"boundary","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A network attacker can relay pairing, obtain an accepted worker token, and make the worker trust the attacker's certificate."},{"id":"F2","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"When the listener is disabled or credentials belong to another process, sandboxed agents can read retained worker credentials and private keys."},{"id":"F3","consequence":"bypass","trigger":"unusual","reason":"The claim secret bypasses credential redaction if it appears in output, although no ordinary path shown logs it."},{"id":"F4","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Expired requests remain listed as pending and accumulate without a retention bound, causing misleading operator decisions and growing stored state."},{"id":"F5","consequence":"test","trigger":"plausible","reason":"The current implementation rejects incorrect claim secrets, but the tests fail to catch a reproduced regression that would disclose a token."}]}